The reported "DNS is down" symptom has no corresponding fault in the live network. Every component of the DNS service chain was verified healthy. The BIND named daemon (PID 29) is running on srv1 (10.5.0.4) and is actively listening on UDP and TCP port 53, bound to both the host's own address and loopback. The jeremysitlab.com zone file is correctly loaded with SOA, NS, A, and wildcard records. Direct DNS queries to 10.5.0.4 — from Office-A (pc2, laptop1), Office-B (pc3, pc1), and localhost — all return correct answers (e.g., srv1.jeremysitlab.com → 10.5.0.4) with no timeout or SERVFAIL. All clients have nameserver 10.5.0.4 correctly set in /etc/resolv.conf. Network reachability to srv1 is fully intact: ping from both office segments shows 0% packet loss with sub-2 ms RTT. The apparent anomaly in systemctl output — both bind9 and named services showing "inactive (dead)" — is a harmless artefact of how the container launches the daemon directly via its startup script rather than via the systemd unit, and is not a fault. The premise of the ticket is incorrect: no DNS outage exists, the service has not crashed, and no device needs to be restarted.
